
  [1mThe fork:[0m does a preference belong to the [3mbrowser[0m or to the [3muser account[0m?

  - [1mlocalStorage (device-scoped)[0m — buys you a working store with zero backend; [38;5;153mgetPref[39m/[38;5;153msetPref[39m can be synchronous, which
    keeps call sites trivial. Costs: preferences don't follow a user to another browser or device, and they're readable
    by any script on the origin. Rules out cross-device sync until you migrate, and a later migration to a server means
    every call site becomes async.
  - [1mServer-side (account-scoped)[0m — preferences follow the user everywhere and survive a cleared browser. Costs: this
    repo's [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is a stub pointing at [38;5;153mhttps://api.example.com/login[39m; there is no real backend, no session token, no
    persistence server-side. You'd be designing against an API that doesn't exist yet, and every read becomes a network
    call with loading and failure states.
  - [1mlocalStorage behind an async interface[0m — same zero-backend start, but [38;5;153mgetPref[39m returns a Promise and the storage
    backend sits behind a swappable adapter. Costs: async ceremony at every call site for something that's currently
    instant, plus an abstraction with exactly one implementation. Buys you a server backend later as an adapter swap
    rather than a rewrite of every caller.

  [1mMy recommendation: localStorage behind an async interface.[0m The async signature is the one decision here that's
  genuinely painful to retrofit — it changes every call site — while the adapter seam itself is maybe twenty lines.
  Given the login flow is explicitly a stub, I read account-scoped preferences as a real eventual direction rather than
  a hypothetical, and paying the small cost now is cheaper than the later sweep. If you're confident this stays a
  device-local app, plain synchronous localStorage is the honest simpler answer.
